# Repo-level secrets, for scripts and CI.
#
#     cp .env.example .env      # .env is git-ignored — paste real values THERE
#
# Every value in this file stays empty. This file is committed, so a token
# pasted here is a token published on GitHub. `.gitleaks.toml` fails CI if that
# happens, but by then the only real fix is rotating the token.
#
# Nothing here is read by the Next.js app, and that is deliberate: the
# backoffice is a static export (`output: "export"`) served from GitHub Pages, so
# any value the app can read ends up in JavaScript that anyone can download.
#
# The rule: a secret NEVER gets a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix. If you catch yourself
# adding one, it is not a secret any more.

# Polar (https://polar.sh) — the merchant of record for VUI Pro.
#
# A server-side token, used by tooling on your machine or in CI: creating and
# updating products, reading orders, or generating a checkout link at build
# time. It is a bearer token for your whole Polar organization, so treat it like
# a password. Never import it from app code, never log it, and rotate it in the
# Polar dashboard if it leaks.
#
# The public checkout link that the /pricing page actually uses is a separate,
# non-secret value: NEXT_PUBLIC_PRO_CHECKOUT_URL in apps/backoffice/.env.example.
POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=""

# Sandbox first. Polar runs a separate sandbox environment with its own tokens,
# so you can create products and run a test checkout without touching real
# money. Set to "sandbox" while you are wiring things up, "production" after.
POLAR_ENVIRONMENT="sandbox"
